WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.003 --> 00:00:02.005
Narrator: From AirPods filled with earwax

00:00:02.005 --> 00:00:03.940
to algae-covered pools

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:06.009
and air vents filled with dust,

00:00:06.009 --> 00:00:07.611
we followed the professionals

00:00:07.611 --> 00:00:11.148
to uncover the satisfying ins and outs

00:00:11.148 --> 00:00:13.049
of how some of the filthiest things

00:00:13.049 --> 00:00:15.552
are professionally deep cleaned.

00:00:19.556 --> 00:00:23.193
Our first expert is Julius
from Phone Fix Craft.

00:00:23.193 --> 00:00:25.829
He shows us how dirty our phones can get

00:00:25.829 --> 00:00:28.598
and how he makes them look brand new.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:49.048
Can you hear that?

00:05:49.048 --> 00:05:53.286
Julius now shares how to bring
clogged AirPods back to life.

00:11:24.050 --> 00:11:26.552
When was the last time that
you went to the dentist?

00:11:26.552 --> 00:11:30.322
Dental hygienist Faye Donald
proves that teeth cleanings

00:11:30.322 --> 00:11:33.125
are nothing to be afraid of.

00:11:33.125 --> 00:11:34.460
Today, I'm going to show you

00:11:34.460 --> 00:11:37.596
how guided biofilm therapy, or GBT,

00:11:37.596 --> 00:11:39.498
works when we professionally clean

00:11:39.498 --> 00:11:42.034
a patient's mouth from start to finish.

00:11:43.102 --> 00:11:47.339
My job as a dental hygienist
is to detect and treat

00:11:47.339 --> 00:11:50.710
but most importantly
prevent periodontal disease.

00:11:50.710 --> 00:11:53.045
Biofilms are highly organized

00:11:53.045 --> 00:11:55.848
microscopic communities of bacteria.

00:11:55.848 --> 00:11:58.117
But it's responsible for bleeding gums,

00:11:58.117 --> 00:12:01.520
for bad breath, for
some dental infections,

00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:03.089
for some of the unsightly deposits

00:12:03.089 --> 00:12:04.890
that we see on our teeth,

00:12:04.890 --> 00:12:06.392
and, of course, it's linked to

00:12:06.392 --> 00:12:10.362
some more serious,
systemic diseases, as well.

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:18.738
So, here we can see plaque
and calculus deposits,

00:12:18.738 --> 00:12:21.140
but more concerning is
the color of the gums,

00:12:21.140 --> 00:12:24.810
which are red, and they're
also puffy and swollen,

00:12:24.810 --> 00:12:27.613
and that would lead us to
believe either gingivitis

00:12:27.613 --> 00:12:30.616
or even periodontal disease
could be present here.

00:12:30.616 --> 00:12:32.818
One of the problems with biofilm

00:12:32.818 --> 00:12:36.155
is that you can't see it
and you can't feel it,

00:12:36.155 --> 00:12:39.759
so I use a dye called Biofilm Discloser,

00:12:39.759 --> 00:12:42.528
which is an organic, colored dye.

00:12:53.539 --> 00:12:55.474
So, when the dye's applied to the teeth,

00:12:55.474 --> 00:12:57.309
which have biofilm on them,

00:12:57.309 --> 00:12:59.512
it changes the color of the biofilm.

00:12:59.512 --> 00:13:01.680
And it's a really clever two-tone dye,

00:13:01.680 --> 00:13:04.950
so it means that it stains early biofilm,

00:13:04.950 --> 00:13:07.353
which is less than three days old, pink,

00:13:07.353 --> 00:13:09.355
and it stains mature biofilm,

00:13:09.355 --> 00:13:11.791
which is more than three days old, blue.

00:13:17.963 --> 00:13:20.232
The patient is shown in the mirror

00:13:20.232 --> 00:13:21.867
the stained biofilm.

00:13:21.867 --> 00:13:24.136
Biofilms are microscopic,

00:13:24.136 --> 00:13:26.906
whereas the plaque is a
biofilm that's matured,

00:13:26.906 --> 00:13:29.942
and it's large enough to
be seen with the naked eye.

00:13:29.942 --> 00:13:32.778
Ideally, what we want is
to disrupt dental biofilm

00:13:32.778 --> 00:13:35.714
before it has the chance
to mature into plaque.

00:13:39.185 --> 00:13:42.288
So, this is where we start
the cleaning process,

00:13:42.288 --> 00:13:44.456
and it is completely different

00:13:44.456 --> 00:13:45.991
to the old-fashioned methods

00:13:45.991 --> 00:13:48.794
of curettage and hand scaling.

00:13:48.794 --> 00:13:51.764
So, here I'm using a
tool called Airflow Max,

00:13:51.764 --> 00:13:56.101
which acts like a warm jet
wash for inside the mouth.

00:13:56.101 --> 00:13:58.270
It uses a combination of warm water

00:13:58.270 --> 00:14:01.941
and tiny soluble and antimicrobial powder.

00:14:01.941 --> 00:14:03.776
Airflow Max will remove

00:14:03.776 --> 00:14:06.345
100% of the biofilm from the teeth

00:14:06.345 --> 00:14:08.080
and all of the surrounding tissues,

00:14:08.080 --> 00:14:09.582
including underneath the gums,

00:14:09.582 --> 00:14:10.883
where it's dark and warm

00:14:10.883 --> 00:14:13.519
and the biofilm really likes to hide.

00:14:25.631 --> 00:14:27.533
I'm also using the Airflow Max

00:14:27.533 --> 00:14:29.134
not just to take the biofilm away,

00:14:29.134 --> 00:14:31.804
but it takes the stains away, as well,

00:14:31.804 --> 00:14:34.840
so the mouth looks and feels
super clean afterwards.

00:14:34.840 --> 00:14:37.343
It's very comfortable for the patient.

00:14:37.343 --> 00:14:40.579
It's gentle, it's
efficient, it's very safe,

00:14:40.579 --> 00:14:44.783
with no abrasion or damage
to the tooth surface at all.

00:14:56.095 --> 00:14:58.597
After we've disrupted all of the biofilm

00:14:58.597 --> 00:15:01.066
and we've removed all of the stains,

00:15:01.066 --> 00:15:04.803
Piezon No-Pain is used

00:15:04.803 --> 00:15:06.906
at this stage,

00:15:06.906 --> 00:15:09.708
and it uses ultrasonic vibrations

00:15:09.708 --> 00:15:13.078
to remove any remaining hard calculus.

00:15:13.078 --> 00:15:16.048
It shaves the calculus off very gently.

00:15:16.048 --> 00:15:17.149
So it's very different

00:15:17.149 --> 00:15:19.718
to the old-fashioned methods
of scraping the teeth,

00:15:19.718 --> 00:15:21.720
which would take calculus away

00:15:21.720 --> 00:15:24.490
but would also scratch
the tooth's surface.

00:15:45.744 --> 00:15:50.616
Stage seven of the GBT process is

00:15:50.616 --> 00:15:52.985
our quality-control stage, really.

00:15:52.985 --> 00:15:56.021
So, here, I would spend a few moments

00:15:56.021 --> 00:15:57.823
checking around the whole mouth

00:15:57.823 --> 00:16:00.092
to ensure that all the
deposits have been removed

00:16:00.092 --> 00:16:03.195
and all the biofilm's been disrupted.

00:16:03.195 --> 00:16:05.431
So, at this stage, I would hand the mirror

00:16:05.431 --> 00:16:06.665
back to my patient

00:16:06.665 --> 00:16:08.934
so they can see the
before-and-after results

00:16:08.934 --> 00:16:11.804
of their guided-biofilm-therapy treatment.

00:16:11.804 --> 00:16:13.172
I usually ask my patients

00:16:13.172 --> 00:16:15.941
to run their tongue over
their teeth at this stage

00:16:15.941 --> 00:16:18.644
to feel how silky-smooth they are

00:16:18.644 --> 00:16:22.047
now that the biofilm and calculus is gone.

00:16:22.047 --> 00:16:24.750
Learning good oral-hygiene habits

00:16:24.750 --> 00:16:26.018
is like learning to swim.

00:16:26.018 --> 00:16:29.288
You have to be shown, so
ask a dental hygienist

00:16:29.288 --> 00:16:31.924
to teach you how to clean
your teeth properly.

00:16:31.924 --> 00:16:34.860
Spend the most time and
concentrate the most efforts

00:16:34.860 --> 00:16:37.329
on the areas that you can't see.

00:16:37.329 --> 00:16:39.231
That's where you'll find the most biofilm.

00:16:39.231 --> 00:16:40.766
So in between the back teeth,

00:16:40.766 --> 00:16:41.867
underneath the gumline,

00:16:41.867 --> 00:16:43.936
around the base of restorations.

00:16:43.936 --> 00:16:46.672
Ah! I love my job!

00:16:46.672 --> 00:16:49.108
I love it so much,

00:16:49.108 --> 00:16:50.809
because I get to put smiles

00:16:50.809 --> 00:16:53.379
on people's faces every single day.

00:16:53.379 --> 00:16:56.181
What other job in the
world gets to do that?

00:17:01.887 --> 00:17:05.824
Next, Miles Laflin transforms
this murky green pool

00:17:05.824 --> 00:17:08.794
back into clean, clear waters.

00:17:10.429 --> 00:17:11.530
Hi, I'm Miles,

00:17:11.530 --> 00:17:12.531
and I'm going to show you

00:17:12.531 --> 00:17:13.932
how I professionally clean a pool

00:17:13.932 --> 00:17:15.401
from start to finish.

00:17:15.401 --> 00:17:20.105
[water bubbling]

00:17:20.105 --> 00:17:22.007
This pool's not looking too good.

00:17:22.007 --> 00:17:23.909
I'm gonna get the submersible
pump out straight away,

00:17:23.909 --> 00:17:24.910
pump the water out,

00:17:24.910 --> 00:17:26.779
and then come back the next day.

00:17:30.215 --> 00:17:32.251
So, here we are, the next day.

00:17:32.251 --> 00:17:34.820
It's empty, but there's a blanket of algae

00:17:34.820 --> 00:17:35.988
all over the pool.

00:17:35.988 --> 00:17:38.490
I'm gonna shovel it all
up, put it in buckets.

00:17:42.728 --> 00:17:44.163
Basically, the green sludge is,

00:17:44.163 --> 00:17:47.099
after a pool's been left for a long time

00:17:47.099 --> 00:17:50.903
without any chlorine or
any sanitizer in the pool,

00:17:50.903 --> 00:17:52.504
you'll get algae buildup.

00:17:52.504 --> 00:17:54.106
Most of them don't smell that bad,

00:17:54.106 --> 00:17:57.142
but when the sludge is
really thick on the bottom

00:17:57.142 --> 00:17:59.878
and you disturb it,

00:17:59.878 --> 00:18:02.147
this obviously has not been
disturbed for a long time,

00:18:02.147 --> 00:18:04.316
the smell that comes out is crazy.

00:18:09.221 --> 00:18:11.356
Basically, when we had the pool empty,

00:18:11.356 --> 00:18:13.492
we realized how many frogs there were.

00:18:13.492 --> 00:18:16.995
I'd never seen so many frogs
in a pool before in my life.

00:18:16.995 --> 00:18:18.797
Like, every scoop, every shovelful,

00:18:18.797 --> 00:18:21.500
there was maybe one or two frogs on it.

00:18:21.500 --> 00:18:22.901
So we had to get them out,

00:18:22.901 --> 00:18:23.836
put them on the side of the pool,

00:18:23.836 --> 00:18:25.704
and let them run off into the bushes.

00:18:37.082 --> 00:18:40.752
So, we used hydrochloric
acid mixed with water,

00:18:40.752 --> 00:18:42.621
so it was diluted down.

00:18:42.621 --> 00:18:44.423
Get out the old Karcher K4.

00:18:44.423 --> 00:18:46.291
Start pressure washing the pool down.

00:18:48.494 --> 00:18:50.062
It's all coming up pretty well.

00:19:03.308 --> 00:19:06.545
At the bottom of the pool
there's a main drain.

00:19:06.545 --> 00:19:08.514
There'd been some work
done around the pool,

00:19:08.514 --> 00:19:10.382
like the slabbing and that.

00:19:10.382 --> 00:19:12.484
Obviously there's a lot of
leaves gone into the pool,

00:19:12.484 --> 00:19:14.486
so a lot of muck at the bottom.

00:19:14.486 --> 00:19:16.455
And just built up and solidified.

00:19:16.455 --> 00:19:17.723
So that had to be broken up.

00:19:17.723 --> 00:19:19.291
Nothing special, it was just, like,

00:19:19.291 --> 00:19:21.193
an old screwdriver from the van.

00:19:21.193 --> 00:19:23.262
That helped to get into it,

00:19:23.262 --> 00:19:27.065
break it up, and then hoover it out.

00:19:27.065 --> 00:19:29.801
[vacuum sucking]

00:20:06.772 --> 00:20:08.073
The dirtiest parts on the pool

00:20:08.073 --> 00:20:10.375
are going to be the filters.

00:20:10.375 --> 00:20:12.144
'Cause basically they have,

00:20:12.144 --> 00:20:13.812
the water runs through the filters

00:20:13.812 --> 00:20:15.981
continuously or on a timer.

00:20:15.981 --> 00:20:18.884
Blue tank is the fill arm with sand in.

00:20:18.884 --> 00:20:20.552
And that keeps the pool water clean.

00:20:20.552 --> 00:20:22.721
What it does is catches all the dirt

00:20:22.721 --> 00:20:25.657
and the stuff that's
floating around in the pool.

00:20:25.657 --> 00:20:28.126
And then once a week,
once every two weeks,

00:20:28.126 --> 00:20:29.294
whenever I come do a maintenance,

00:20:29.294 --> 00:20:32.464
do a backwash, and that
gets rid of all the dirt

00:20:32.464 --> 00:20:35.000
and anything that's been
floating around in the pool.

00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:37.369
New sand in, cut it open.

00:20:42.474 --> 00:20:45.277
I'll do five bags of sand

00:20:45.277 --> 00:20:47.045
so there's 24-inch fill-up.

00:20:50.148 --> 00:20:52.050
Slip the lid back on.

00:20:52.050 --> 00:20:55.320
[water gurgling]

00:20:55.320 --> 00:20:56.922
When I do the chemicals,

00:20:56.922 --> 00:20:58.724
they all look the same, chlorine as well.

00:20:58.724 --> 00:21:01.026
It's all just like sort of white powder.

00:21:01.026 --> 00:21:02.894
But basically I'm just
balancing the chemicals

00:21:02.894 --> 00:21:06.565
on the swimming pools when
I'm doing the maintenance.

00:21:06.565 --> 00:21:08.033
A few bags of salt needed.

00:21:09.001 --> 00:21:10.602
Three 25-kilo bags.

00:21:13.939 --> 00:21:16.675
[water bubbling]

00:21:23.615 --> 00:21:25.784
Basically, what happens
when you pour it in,

00:21:25.784 --> 00:21:27.819
it goes through the system

00:21:27.819 --> 00:21:29.688
and runs through a salt cell,

00:21:29.688 --> 00:21:31.690
which is just a chlorine generator.

00:21:31.690 --> 00:21:33.191
So as it's running through the salt cell,

00:21:33.191 --> 00:21:35.460
it uses a process called electrolysis.

00:21:35.460 --> 00:21:36.862
I think I said it right.

00:21:38.630 --> 00:21:41.300
Basically just turning
into chlorine for the pool.

00:21:41.300 --> 00:21:43.201
Get a brush out, brush it all around

00:21:43.201 --> 00:21:45.037
so it's not just sitting on the floor,

00:21:47.406 --> 00:21:49.041
and that's that job done.

00:21:52.911 --> 00:21:56.348
This was definitely the
worst pool I've worked on

00:21:56.348 --> 00:21:58.750
in terms of how dirty it was

00:21:58.750 --> 00:22:00.619
and how much algae there was.

00:22:00.619 --> 00:22:03.588
And smell. The smell was just ridiculous.

00:22:03.588 --> 00:22:06.058
It was unbearable at some times.

00:22:06.058 --> 00:22:07.526
It didn't take me all day,

00:22:07.526 --> 00:22:08.560
it didn't take too long.

00:22:08.560 --> 00:22:09.795
I know it looks like a lot of mess,

00:22:09.795 --> 00:22:12.230
but it went pretty well.

00:22:12.230 --> 00:22:13.865
A lot of people say to me, you know,

00:22:13.865 --> 00:22:15.000
"Don't you ever just feel like

00:22:15.000 --> 00:22:16.034
just jumping in the pool?"

00:22:16.034 --> 00:22:17.969
I mean, on a really hot day I do.

00:22:17.969 --> 00:22:19.438
But other than that,

00:22:19.438 --> 00:22:21.073
I'm not really that bothered about it.

00:22:27.946 --> 00:22:31.850
Miles now revives a hot tub
that is perfect for a spa day.

00:22:34.519 --> 00:22:35.420
Today I'm going to show you

00:22:35.420 --> 00:22:37.789
how to professionally clean a hot tub.

00:22:39.391 --> 00:22:40.959
All right, this is probably

00:22:40.959 --> 00:22:42.928
the worst hot tub I've had to work on.

00:22:42.928 --> 00:22:45.764
There's literally slugs, spiders,

00:22:45.764 --> 00:22:47.499
the water's black,

00:22:47.499 --> 00:22:49.868
and the smell is just disgusting.

00:22:56.641 --> 00:22:58.076
So, the first thing I've
done when I got there

00:22:58.076 --> 00:23:00.946
was taking the cover
off for the actual job,

00:23:00.946 --> 00:23:02.948
which, you had to take the cover off,

00:23:02.948 --> 00:23:04.916
inspect it, see how bad it was.

00:23:04.916 --> 00:23:07.452
We took that off and
stood it up on the grass,

00:23:07.452 --> 00:23:10.756
and that got jet washed and brushed down.

00:23:10.756 --> 00:23:12.958
And there's a lot of creases in the cover,

00:23:12.958 --> 00:23:15.193
so there's slugs in there as well,

00:23:15.193 --> 00:23:18.096
still trying to hide and
sort of stay in their home,

00:23:18.096 --> 00:23:19.664
but they all got cleared out.

00:23:19.664 --> 00:23:22.601
So it was mostly water with the jet wash,

00:23:22.601 --> 00:23:26.104
and then you had a little
bit of cleaning solution.

00:23:26.104 --> 00:23:29.007
With the cover, that took
around an hour to clean.

00:23:36.281 --> 00:23:38.984
On the side there's a
lot of black markings,

00:23:38.984 --> 00:23:40.752
like slime and dirt,

00:23:40.752 --> 00:23:45.157
and it's still a lot slugs
sort of slithering around.

00:23:45.157 --> 00:23:47.125
Like, 100 slugs on this hot tub.

00:23:47.125 --> 00:23:48.827
And, yeah, the guy said

00:23:48.827 --> 00:23:51.062
he hadn't used it for, like, 10 years.

00:23:51.062 --> 00:23:53.031
So I just started off with a jet wash,

00:23:53.031 --> 00:23:55.400
and they sort of got jet
washed away at first.

00:23:55.400 --> 00:23:57.436
There was so many, and
to be honest with you,

00:23:57.436 --> 00:24:00.238
I don't really fancy picking up slugs, so.

00:24:08.013 --> 00:24:09.347
So, the white tube there,

00:24:09.347 --> 00:24:11.850
that's connected to my submersible pump,

00:24:11.850 --> 00:24:14.085
which I'm using to pump water

00:24:14.085 --> 00:24:15.754
from the hot tub down the drain.

00:24:16.788 --> 00:24:18.890
So I've got the broom
out, 'cause you've got,

00:24:18.890 --> 00:24:21.460
where you sit down in
the hot tub is quite,

00:24:21.460 --> 00:24:23.795
it's a bit like a bowl, so
it'll hold a lot of water,

00:24:23.795 --> 00:24:24.763
and what I'm just doing is

00:24:24.763 --> 00:24:26.097
I'm just pushing it into the middle

00:24:26.097 --> 00:24:29.901
so the submersible pump
can drain it out easier.

00:24:39.644 --> 00:24:41.680
At the bottom of the tub,
you just had a little bit

00:24:41.680 --> 00:24:44.349
of dirty water and a little bit of grit.

00:24:55.126 --> 00:24:56.061
So, underneath here,

00:24:56.061 --> 00:24:57.562
you've got the main circulating pump.

00:24:57.562 --> 00:24:59.831
You've got the heater for the hot tub.

00:24:59.831 --> 00:25:02.234
All the pump equipment's
kept underneath the hot tub.

00:25:02.234 --> 00:25:04.369
So, I pulled the panel back,

00:25:04.369 --> 00:25:05.971
which all needed to be
cleaned out as well,

00:25:05.971 --> 00:25:07.873
and I used the Hoover to get all of that.

00:25:17.849 --> 00:25:20.552
So, a hot-tub flush is a chemical,

00:25:20.552 --> 00:25:23.822
which you will pour in through

00:25:23.822 --> 00:25:25.724
where the filters are usually set,

00:25:25.724 --> 00:25:29.995
and then you turn on all
the jets in the hot tub.

00:25:29.995 --> 00:25:31.930
So, yeah, once you put
the hot-tub flush in

00:25:31.930 --> 00:25:35.934
and it starts reacting with all the fats

00:25:35.934 --> 00:25:38.970
and anything that's
caught up in the tubes,

00:25:38.970 --> 00:25:41.907
inside the hot tub, it creates a foam,

00:25:41.907 --> 00:25:43.508
and then you can also see, in the foam,

00:25:43.508 --> 00:25:44.442
you can see the dirt

00:25:44.442 --> 00:25:47.379
which is coming out of the pipes as well.

00:25:47.379 --> 00:25:49.147
So you can see it's working.

00:25:55.720 --> 00:25:58.623
Once it bubbles up, you can get in there

00:25:58.623 --> 00:26:00.425
and just net them out so they don't --

00:26:00.425 --> 00:26:02.827
'cause what happens is it'll
start sticking to the sides,

00:26:02.827 --> 00:26:05.997
'cause it's like an acrylic material.

00:26:05.997 --> 00:26:08.099
So it will stick to the sides,

00:26:08.099 --> 00:26:09.601
and if you don't scoop it out,

00:26:09.601 --> 00:26:12.504
you'll have to then scrub the sides again.

00:26:12.504 --> 00:26:15.173
So it's just easier just to get in there,

00:26:15.173 --> 00:26:18.443
net now the foamy bits and
all of the dirt on the foam,

00:26:18.443 --> 00:26:20.412
and it'll make it easier for yourself.

00:26:26.785 --> 00:26:28.987
So, I've got this hot-tub filter here,

00:26:28.987 --> 00:26:30.755
and it's looking really black.

00:26:30.755 --> 00:26:32.257
There's a lot of dirt all over it,

00:26:32.257 --> 00:26:33.458
and I'm going to give it a rinse down

00:26:33.458 --> 00:26:36.027
just to clear it so it's
filtering properly again.

00:26:41.466 --> 00:26:43.501
Most pools have sand filters,

00:26:43.501 --> 00:26:46.504
whereas hot tubs have cartridge filters.

00:26:46.504 --> 00:26:47.639
So what happens is, obviously,

00:26:47.639 --> 00:26:50.575
while the filters are
collecting the small particles,

00:26:50.575 --> 00:26:53.178
if you don't take them out and rinse them,

00:26:53.178 --> 00:26:55.880
your water will start to go cloudy.

00:26:55.880 --> 00:26:57.882
So it's basically just
dirt and then obviously

00:26:57.882 --> 00:27:02.887
anything that comes off
your body, like dead skin.

00:27:02.887 --> 00:27:05.190
Anything can get caught
up in the filter as well.

00:27:05.190 --> 00:27:07.959
So you can rinse them down
with your garden hose,

00:27:07.959 --> 00:27:10.495
and then you can spray
them with filter cleaner,

00:27:10.495 --> 00:27:13.131
which just disinfects any
bacteria that's on them.

00:27:15.934 --> 00:27:17.636
And then put them straight
back into the hot tub,

00:27:17.636 --> 00:27:20.405
and then they're ready to go again.

00:27:28.813 --> 00:27:32.350
On hot tubs you can get a
little floating dispenser,

00:27:32.350 --> 00:27:33.985
and it just floats around on the water,

00:27:33.985 --> 00:27:37.455
just slowly releasing chlorine or bromine.

00:27:43.161 --> 00:27:45.997
There's also a chemical test kit,

00:27:45.997 --> 00:27:47.999
and on the left will be to test

00:27:47.999 --> 00:27:50.301
for the bromine or chlorine level,

00:27:50.301 --> 00:27:54.372
and on the right I am
testing for the pH level.

00:27:54.372 --> 00:27:56.374
You're looking for the middle color,

00:27:56.374 --> 00:27:58.043
which is around 7.2.

00:28:02.847 --> 00:28:05.216
So, with the hot tub, it was looking,

00:28:05.216 --> 00:28:06.217
after I'd finished with it,

00:28:06.217 --> 00:28:08.820
it was looking brand
new, nice and blue again.

00:28:09.688 --> 00:28:11.356
The customer's really happy with it,

00:28:11.356 --> 00:28:14.325
and it just looked like a new hot tub

00:28:14.325 --> 00:28:17.462
rather than what it did,
like, five hours ago.

00:28:17.462 --> 00:28:20.031
That was definitely the worst
hot tub I've ever worked on,

00:28:20.031 --> 00:28:22.600
and actually seeing the final product

00:28:22.600 --> 00:28:24.335
with nice and blue water in it again,

00:28:24.335 --> 00:28:26.771
nice and clear, and
everything working on there,

00:28:26.771 --> 00:28:28.206
I was pretty happy with that.

00:28:33.278 --> 00:28:34.412
For his last trick,

00:28:34.412 --> 00:28:38.650
witness Miles as he takes
this pool from green to clean.

00:28:39.017 --> 00:28:41.119
Today, I'm going to show
you a few different pools

00:28:41.119 --> 00:28:43.354
and how I clean the bottom of the pool.

00:28:48.860 --> 00:28:51.730
Grandma's pool definitely
was the most challenging.

00:28:52.731 --> 00:28:53.898
The sludge on the bottom,

00:28:53.898 --> 00:28:57.302
it was, like, 6 inches of
sludge across the whole pool.

00:28:57.302 --> 00:28:58.770
It looked very abandoned

00:28:58.770 --> 00:29:02.407
because obviously it'd
been left for 15 years.

00:29:02.407 --> 00:29:04.476
So that was when I
first got into the pool.

00:29:04.476 --> 00:29:05.944
Obviously there was a lot,

00:29:05.944 --> 00:29:08.179
the water had been drained
down quite a lot there.

00:29:08.179 --> 00:29:10.248
But as I stepped in, it was really soft.

00:29:10.248 --> 00:29:11.883
Branches may have fell off of

00:29:11.883 --> 00:29:15.587
the bushes that were around the pool

00:29:15.587 --> 00:29:16.888
and then just sort of,

00:29:16.888 --> 00:29:19.057
they could have started
growing bigger as well

00:29:19.057 --> 00:29:20.825
while they were in the pool as well.

00:29:20.825 --> 00:29:22.894
So we had to shovel it into buckets,

00:29:22.894 --> 00:29:25.497
and then someone would
put it in a wheelbarrow

00:29:25.497 --> 00:29:28.533
and take it down to the heap
at the end of the garden,

00:29:28.533 --> 00:29:32.003
and it just created a
massive pile of just sludge.

00:29:32.003 --> 00:29:34.572
I'd say there was probably a ton

00:29:34.572 --> 00:29:36.674
or maybe 2 tons of sludge.

00:29:37.709 --> 00:29:40.345
So, yeah, draining that down

00:29:40.345 --> 00:29:43.148
and scooping all the
sludge out of that one

00:29:43.148 --> 00:29:45.150
and just the time it took,

00:29:45.150 --> 00:29:46.851
yeah, that was definitely the hardest one.

00:29:46.851 --> 00:29:48.887
But we got it there in the end.

00:30:07.105 --> 00:30:10.608
Oh, my God. So, recently,
I've been to a job,

00:30:10.608 --> 00:30:13.444
and the pool has been left
for, like, eight years,

00:30:13.444 --> 00:30:15.914
but the water, weirdly, was clear,

00:30:15.914 --> 00:30:19.384
but the algae in there
was really blanket-like,

00:30:19.384 --> 00:30:21.052
and there was loads of it,

00:30:21.052 --> 00:30:22.720
and I could move it around,

00:30:22.720 --> 00:30:24.255
and you could see it looked really weird,

00:30:24.255 --> 00:30:28.693
like an underwater sort
of forest of algae.

00:30:28.693 --> 00:30:31.029
It was really bad.

00:30:31.029 --> 00:30:34.699
And that pool definitely
had to be drained down.

00:30:34.699 --> 00:30:36.968
OK, so when I first go to a pool

00:30:36.968 --> 00:30:39.070
and I see that it's green,

00:30:39.070 --> 00:30:42.073
what I'll do first is
get a pool brush out,

00:30:42.073 --> 00:30:44.309
and then I'll brush the whole pool,

00:30:44.309 --> 00:30:47.979
all the surfaces, disturbing
all the algae off the surface.

00:30:47.979 --> 00:30:50.882
Normally algae will just break up.

00:30:50.882 --> 00:30:54.919
Like, you put chemicals
in, you brush the pool,

00:30:54.919 --> 00:30:57.488
and it will just break up
into really small particles,

00:30:57.488 --> 00:30:59.190
and then you can just Hoover it.

00:30:59.190 --> 00:31:02.460
But this type of algae was just really

00:31:02.460 --> 00:31:06.764
stringy and just solid together.

00:31:08.166 --> 00:31:10.034
Yeah, it was a weird one.

00:31:10.034 --> 00:31:12.637
And then, again, there's
sludge on the floor.

00:31:12.637 --> 00:31:16.007
We're just brushing the sludge to a pile

00:31:16.007 --> 00:31:17.675
and then scooping it out.

00:31:29.187 --> 00:31:32.490
That pool had been left just,

00:31:32.490 --> 00:31:34.959
gone a couple of days without chlorine,

00:31:34.959 --> 00:31:39.397
and because it was indoor,

00:31:39.397 --> 00:31:40.865
sort of in a greenhouse type,

00:31:40.865 --> 00:31:44.135
so it got really hot in
there when the sun comes out,

00:31:44.135 --> 00:31:46.204
and just the algae just attached itself

00:31:46.204 --> 00:31:48.706
to the tiles so badly.

00:31:48.706 --> 00:31:51.309
It looked like it had been left for years.

00:31:51.309 --> 00:31:54.045
People thought they were
actually green tiles

00:31:54.045 --> 00:31:56.948
on the steps. And it was
really tough on the tiles.

00:31:56.948 --> 00:31:59.984
It needed something quite
aggressive to get it off.

00:31:59.984 --> 00:32:03.821
And then what I got was
just some hydrochloric acid,

00:32:03.821 --> 00:32:07.992
mixed it with water, and
sprayed it over the tiles,

00:32:07.992 --> 00:32:11.896
and it just started
reacting with the algae

00:32:11.896 --> 00:32:13.998
by bubbling up.

00:32:13.998 --> 00:32:15.667
And then I started brushing it in

00:32:15.667 --> 00:32:17.769
to disturb it all off the surface.

00:32:26.945 --> 00:32:29.614
Then I got the hose

00:32:29.614 --> 00:32:32.150
and started watering it
down, and it all cleaned off.

00:32:32.150 --> 00:32:33.985
Come up nice and blue.

00:32:33.985 --> 00:32:35.586
And then rinsed it down.

00:32:35.586 --> 00:32:38.656
And that literally took, like, 15 minutes.

00:32:47.632 --> 00:32:48.967
The woman who owned the pool,

00:32:48.967 --> 00:32:53.404
she only called us to come
and open the pool in August.

00:32:53.404 --> 00:32:56.507
Had the whole summer just
sitting there doing nothing.

00:32:56.507 --> 00:32:58.810
So we took the cover off,

00:32:58.810 --> 00:33:01.879
and there was mosquito larvae in the pool,

00:33:01.879 --> 00:33:04.949
and there was mosquitoes
flying everywhere.

00:33:04.949 --> 00:33:07.819
Literally so bad I had to
run away from the pool.

00:33:07.819 --> 00:33:12.690
And she actually didn't
want this pool emptied,

00:33:12.690 --> 00:33:14.158
she just wanted to try and get it around

00:33:14.158 --> 00:33:16.361
with chemicals and get some chemicals out.

00:33:16.361 --> 00:33:20.531
Shock and chlorine granules
were used on this one.

00:33:20.531 --> 00:33:23.601
So, when I shock a pool,
that basically kills off

00:33:23.601 --> 00:33:26.037
all the algae and all the
contaminants in the pool.

00:33:26.037 --> 00:33:29.207
Basically what it does is
combine all the small particles,

00:33:29.207 --> 00:33:32.410
and what it relies on is
no movement in the water.

00:33:32.410 --> 00:33:34.078
So it's combining all the particles,

00:33:34.078 --> 00:33:35.279
and then they become heavy,

00:33:35.279 --> 00:33:36.781
and then they just sink to the bottom.

00:33:36.781 --> 00:33:38.649
Yeah, after a few days, come back,

00:33:38.649 --> 00:33:40.885
and it was like, the
water was crystal clear.

00:33:40.885 --> 00:33:42.653
Just all the debris and
everything was just on the bottom.

00:33:42.653 --> 00:33:44.355
And I came in and just Hoovered it up,

00:33:44.355 --> 00:33:45.723
and it was just crystal clear.

00:33:45.723 --> 00:33:47.558
Ready-to-go sort of thing.

00:34:04.876 --> 00:34:06.744
So, if you've got an outdoor pool,

00:34:06.744 --> 00:34:08.279
you're going to want to maintain it

00:34:08.279 --> 00:34:09.881
once a week at least.

00:34:09.881 --> 00:34:11.816
Nah, so, I get a lot
of people do say to me,

00:34:11.816 --> 00:34:14.452
"Why don't you drain
the pool before winter?"

00:34:14.452 --> 00:34:18.256
But what it is, is you've got
to take into consideration

00:34:18.256 --> 00:34:19.524
the structure of the pool.

00:34:19.524 --> 00:34:21.659
So if you have no water in there,

00:34:21.659 --> 00:34:24.662
if there's water in the
ground surrounding the pool,

00:34:24.662 --> 00:34:28.633
it can pressurize the walls
and then cause damage.

00:34:28.633 --> 00:34:31.269
You have to keep water in the pool.

00:34:31.269 --> 00:34:34.372
What you do have to do is check your pool

00:34:34.372 --> 00:34:37.708
at least once a month and
add the correct chemicals,

00:34:37.708 --> 00:34:40.144
and then when you come
to open it in summer,

00:34:40.144 --> 00:34:42.246
it's going to be nice and clear still.

00:34:45.683 --> 00:34:48.052
Your allergies will thank you after 

00:34:48.052 --> 00:34:51.589
47 years of dust is cleaned out of this vent.

00:34:52.123 --> 00:34:54.125
Today, I'm going to show you how I clean

00:34:54.125 --> 00:34:57.161
47 years of dust out of vents.

00:35:03.701 --> 00:35:04.902
As soon as we walked in,

00:35:04.902 --> 00:35:06.604
we see the old vents,

00:35:06.604 --> 00:35:08.673
the furnace, how old it was.

00:35:08.673 --> 00:35:11.075
Before even opening any of the vents up,

00:35:11.075 --> 00:35:12.977
I knew how bad it was gonna be.

00:35:12.977 --> 00:35:15.847
You know, he said 47 years of dust,

00:35:15.847 --> 00:35:18.549
probably over 100 years, to be honest.

00:35:21.152 --> 00:35:25.356
If you look at a vent and you see dust

00:35:25.356 --> 00:35:27.558
on the actual vent,

00:35:27.558 --> 00:35:29.293
that's a clear sign that

00:35:29.293 --> 00:35:31.329
the ducts have probably never been cleaned

00:35:31.329 --> 00:35:33.531
and they're pretty filthy inside.

00:35:33.531 --> 00:35:35.967
We like to start on the
very top of the house

00:35:35.967 --> 00:35:37.535
and work our way down.

00:35:37.535 --> 00:35:39.370
That tool that we're using,

00:35:39.370 --> 00:35:41.439
we call it whiskers.

00:35:41.439 --> 00:35:43.441
The whiskers are just made out of rubber.

00:35:43.441 --> 00:35:45.776
The end of it's attached
to an air compressor,

00:35:45.776 --> 00:35:49.147
and you have air just
coming out through the rod,

00:35:49.147 --> 00:35:51.282
out the little whisker things.

00:35:51.282 --> 00:35:55.086
[whiskers whirling]

00:35:59.390 --> 00:36:01.425
So, what you're looking at right now

00:36:01.425 --> 00:36:04.829
is just me pulling chunks of dust out

00:36:04.829 --> 00:36:06.330
that have probably been in there

00:36:06.330 --> 00:36:08.099
for over 100 years.

00:36:08.099 --> 00:36:09.667
There was too much dust

00:36:09.667 --> 00:36:11.302
to be putting it in the vacuum,

00:36:12.203 --> 00:36:15.106
so I literally went in there with my hands

00:36:15.106 --> 00:36:18.476
and grabbed probably,
like, 5 pounds of dust.

00:36:18.476 --> 00:36:19.477
Isn't it gnarly?

00:36:19.477 --> 00:36:22.213
Like, look at how much dust that is.

00:36:22.213 --> 00:36:25.149
It's just so sick, honestly.

00:36:36.360 --> 00:36:39.096
This is the back of the dryer,

00:36:39.096 --> 00:36:41.399
and you guys can pretty much see

00:36:41.399 --> 00:36:44.168
there's an endless amount
of lint coming out.

00:36:45.469 --> 00:36:47.171
There's a few reasons why you should get

00:36:47.171 --> 00:36:48.472
your dryer vent cleaned.

00:36:48.472 --> 00:36:50.608
It's a huge fire hazard,

00:36:50.608 --> 00:36:53.311
and your clothes will
dry more efficiently.

00:36:53.311 --> 00:36:55.079
It's well worth the money

00:36:55.079 --> 00:36:57.915
to just pay to get it cleaned every year.

00:37:09.260 --> 00:37:12.930
The vacuum is on throughout
the whole process,

00:37:14.098 --> 00:37:15.266
and what we're doing is blowing

00:37:15.266 --> 00:37:16.634
all that dust and debris

00:37:16.634 --> 00:37:19.270
closer and closer to the vacuum.

00:37:26.877 --> 00:37:29.614
This house in particular had 18 vents.

00:37:29.614 --> 00:37:30.681
Most of the time,

00:37:30.681 --> 00:37:34.485
I think probably 75% of the time,

00:37:34.485 --> 00:37:39.123
the houses that we go to
have never been cleaned.

00:37:39.123 --> 00:37:40.925
People just don't think about it.

00:37:40.925 --> 00:37:43.160
You know, getting your air ducts cleaned

00:37:43.160 --> 00:37:44.629
isn't, like, a regular thought

00:37:44.629 --> 00:37:47.031
that comes to your head.

00:37:47.031 --> 00:37:50.034
The dust you typically
see in your vents is,

00:37:50.034 --> 00:37:53.938
most of it is actually human skin cells.

00:37:53.938 --> 00:37:56.040
Yeah, it's pretty gnarly.

00:38:00.244 --> 00:38:02.280
So, right now we're done,

00:38:02.280 --> 00:38:06.284
and everything's pretty much good to go.

00:38:06.284 --> 00:38:08.786
You're pretty much going to see

00:38:08.786 --> 00:38:11.856
a brand-new home, honestly.

00:38:11.856 --> 00:38:14.325
You're going to feel much better.

00:38:14.325 --> 00:38:15.993
Your health is going to be better.

00:38:15.993 --> 00:38:18.863
I actually got a phone
call after that job,

00:38:18.863 --> 00:38:23.000
and they said all their
allergies went away.

00:38:23.000 --> 00:38:25.736
It's just a huge health benefit, honestly.

00:38:26.604 --> 00:38:29.540
You might not notice the small stuff,

00:38:29.540 --> 00:38:30.941
but your body does.

00:38:36.514 --> 00:38:39.450
Clogged gutters can wreak havoc on a home,

00:38:39.450 --> 00:38:42.920
but The Gutter Guyz can
clear them out with ease.

00:38:43.921 --> 00:38:47.425
I'm Broden, and I own a business

00:38:47.425 --> 00:38:49.226
in Connecticut called The Gutter Guyz.

00:38:49.226 --> 00:38:51.329
Today I'm going to show
you how to clean gutters

00:38:51.329 --> 00:38:53.631
with wet and dry debris.

00:39:01.238 --> 00:39:03.874
So, when we clean a
gutter with wet debris,

00:39:03.874 --> 00:39:05.176
nine times out of 10,

00:39:05.176 --> 00:39:06.744
we want to clean it by hand.

00:39:06.744 --> 00:39:09.046
The reason why is when it's wet,

00:39:09.046 --> 00:39:10.881
it typically is very muddy

00:39:10.881 --> 00:39:12.583
and there's a lot of what we call

00:39:12.583 --> 00:39:15.886
shingle dust or grit in the gutters,

00:39:15.886 --> 00:39:17.188
and it's literally a mud.

00:39:17.188 --> 00:39:19.523
And when we blow them when they're wet,

00:39:19.523 --> 00:39:21.459
it makes a muddy mess all over the house.

00:39:21.459 --> 00:39:23.661
We want to clean them
by hand for that reason.

00:39:25.696 --> 00:39:28.399
So, that is a gutter that is completely

00:39:28.399 --> 00:39:30.968
jammed with leaves and debris,

00:39:30.968 --> 00:39:34.705
and the downspout was completely covered.

00:39:34.705 --> 00:39:37.408
When we released the
debris out of the downspout

00:39:37.408 --> 00:39:38.843
and got that gutter cleaned,

00:39:38.843 --> 00:39:41.779
all of that rainwater
that was in the gutter

00:39:41.779 --> 00:39:44.415
just started to flow very heavily

00:39:44.415 --> 00:39:46.550
right down the downspout, as it should.

00:39:49.653 --> 00:39:52.089
So, oftentimes when we
do a gutter cleaning,

00:39:52.089 --> 00:39:53.958
not only do we find leaves,

00:39:53.958 --> 00:39:56.627
but we find plants so big

00:39:56.627 --> 00:39:58.229
that we actually call them trees,

00:39:58.229 --> 00:40:00.264
sometimes, growing right out of a gutter.

00:40:00.264 --> 00:40:03.200
We're actually pulling out a big plant,

00:40:03.200 --> 00:40:04.902
and you can see the whole root system

00:40:04.902 --> 00:40:06.270
come right out with it.

00:40:07.872 --> 00:40:10.074
Certain types of trees give off

00:40:10.074 --> 00:40:12.309
these little things that come off the tree

00:40:12.309 --> 00:40:13.878
and will grow another tree,

00:40:13.878 --> 00:40:16.280
and when those land in the gutter,

00:40:16.280 --> 00:40:19.650
it's a perfect environment
for that to grow.

00:40:19.650 --> 00:40:21.252
It's a classic sign that your gutters

00:40:21.252 --> 00:40:22.720
have not been cleaned in years.

00:40:22.720 --> 00:40:25.890
It also puts a significant
amount of weight

00:40:25.890 --> 00:40:28.426
from the whole root system
growing in the gutter

00:40:28.426 --> 00:40:30.628
on the gutter, which in turn

00:40:30.628 --> 00:40:33.597
will pull that gutter
off of the fascia board,

00:40:33.597 --> 00:40:36.066
which is actually the
board behind the gutter

00:40:36.066 --> 00:40:37.701
that the gutter is mounted to.

00:40:39.870 --> 00:40:44.442
Wet debris is usually
just saturated with water.

00:40:44.442 --> 00:40:46.644
Water will overflow at the gutter

00:40:46.644 --> 00:40:48.579
and get into your basement

00:40:48.579 --> 00:40:51.315
and create cracks in your foundation.

00:40:51.315 --> 00:40:53.751
Once water does find its
way into your basement,

00:40:53.751 --> 00:40:55.186
it wreaks havoc.

00:40:57.621 --> 00:40:59.323
Here, the water that's flowing

00:40:59.323 --> 00:41:01.592
out of the downspout at the bottom

00:41:01.592 --> 00:41:04.195
is the end result that we are looking for.

00:41:04.195 --> 00:41:06.831
So that water will flow down and out,

00:41:06.831 --> 00:41:08.265
as it properly should.

00:41:12.837 --> 00:41:14.805
When we see dry debris,

00:41:14.805 --> 00:41:16.774
the reason why that debris is dry

00:41:16.774 --> 00:41:19.109
is because there has not been any rain

00:41:19.109 --> 00:41:21.712
in the past, I would say, week or so.

00:41:21.712 --> 00:41:24.215
So when we clean a gutter
that has dry debris,

00:41:24.215 --> 00:41:26.383
oftentimes we will use a hand blower

00:41:26.383 --> 00:41:27.885
and blow those gutters out,

00:41:27.885 --> 00:41:29.587
because it does a really good job

00:41:29.587 --> 00:41:31.589
of just getting everything
out of the gutter,

00:41:31.589 --> 00:41:34.492
whether it be leaves or
sticks or curly stuff

00:41:34.492 --> 00:41:36.026
that comes off in the spring.

00:41:36.026 --> 00:41:37.394
Depending on the pitch of the roof,

00:41:37.394 --> 00:41:38.696
we will get on that roof,

00:41:38.696 --> 00:41:39.964
start our hand blower,

00:41:39.964 --> 00:41:42.066
and walk across that roofline

00:41:42.066 --> 00:41:44.401
hand-blowing out all of the debris

00:41:44.401 --> 00:41:45.936
out of that gutter.

00:41:47.505 --> 00:41:50.274
Sometimes the debris is very compact

00:41:50.274 --> 00:41:51.275
and it's thick.

00:41:51.275 --> 00:41:54.845
That's a classic sign of
maybe a couple of years

00:41:54.845 --> 00:41:57.681
that somebody had not had
their gutters cleaned.

00:41:58.616 --> 00:42:00.251
We stick to one type of ladder,

00:42:00.251 --> 00:42:01.752
and that's an aluminum ladder.

00:42:01.752 --> 00:42:04.288
The aluminum ladder does
conduct electricity,

00:42:04.288 --> 00:42:05.689
so it's very important we stay

00:42:05.689 --> 00:42:06.690
away from power lines

00:42:06.690 --> 00:42:09.093
when we are moving aluminum ladders.

00:42:09.093 --> 00:42:11.762
We could get severely electrocuted.

00:42:11.762 --> 00:42:13.030
It's certainly deadly,

00:42:13.030 --> 00:42:16.600
which is why we mark the
power-line areas with flags

00:42:16.600 --> 00:42:18.469
before we start a gutter cleaning.

00:42:18.469 --> 00:42:20.070
Because you're looking
where you're walking,

00:42:20.070 --> 00:42:22.039
so you don't look up
to see the power lines.

00:42:22.039 --> 00:42:23.440
So it's very important for us to have

00:42:23.440 --> 00:42:26.210
the reminder that power lines are there.

00:42:26.210 --> 00:42:29.914
We will often drop a
penny down the downspout

00:42:29.914 --> 00:42:32.516
just to know that that
downspout's free and clear,

00:42:32.516 --> 00:42:34.552
because we can see and hear it go

00:42:34.552 --> 00:42:37.054
all the way through and
back out at the bottom

00:42:37.054 --> 00:42:39.490
and we know that we're in good shape.

00:42:42.493 --> 00:42:44.528
The best advice I can give a homeowner

00:42:44.528 --> 00:42:46.964
is to be proactive, not reactive.

00:42:46.964 --> 00:42:49.333
Typically when you're
reactive, it's too late.

00:42:49.333 --> 00:42:50.434
Damage has been done,

00:42:50.434 --> 00:42:52.403
the water may already be in your basement,

00:42:52.403 --> 00:42:54.238
it may already be in your home,

00:42:54.238 --> 00:42:59.176
and it's just not a good
way to maintain your house.

00:42:59.176 --> 00:43:03.013
Water is seriously
detrimental to your house,

00:43:03.013 --> 00:43:05.950
and the main cause of
water is from your gutters.

00:43:05.950 --> 00:43:08.986
So if you keep the gutters free and clear

00:43:08.986 --> 00:43:11.188
and you do consistent cleaning,

00:43:11.188 --> 00:43:13.123
whether it be two or three times per year,

00:43:13.123 --> 00:43:14.425
which we recommend,

00:43:14.425 --> 00:43:16.360
you will never have any issues

00:43:16.360 --> 00:43:17.661
with water in your basement

00:43:17.661 --> 00:43:19.363
or ruining your flower beds

00:43:19.363 --> 00:43:22.199
or rotting out the fascia
and soffits of your home.

00:43:22.199 --> 00:43:23.601
Just stay on top of gutter cleaning.

00:43:28.372 --> 00:43:30.808
Step up your spring cleaning with Aj

00:43:30.808 --> 00:43:33.110
as he pressure washes years of dirt.

00:43:37.414 --> 00:43:38.616
My name's Aj Joyner,

00:43:38.616 --> 00:43:41.251
owner of Advanced Pressure Washing, LLC.

00:43:41.251 --> 00:43:42.219
So, today we're gonna show you

00:43:42.219 --> 00:43:44.555
how we clean the exterior of a client's

00:43:44.555 --> 00:43:46.523
dirty home, step by step.

00:43:48.359 --> 00:43:51.161
We, in general, we
offer exterior cleaning,

00:43:51.161 --> 00:43:53.263
but the main services
in the residential field

00:43:53.263 --> 00:43:56.700
are house washing, gutter clean-out,

00:43:56.700 --> 00:43:57.968
concrete cleaning.

00:43:57.968 --> 00:43:59.903
Those are the main-ticket items

00:43:59.903 --> 00:44:02.406
that we solicit to the
residential clients.

00:44:08.946 --> 00:44:12.416
The condition of the fence
structurally is great,

00:44:12.416 --> 00:44:13.250
but as you can see,

00:44:13.250 --> 00:44:14.985
it's covered in mildew and algae.

00:44:14.985 --> 00:44:16.253
Wood is very particular

00:44:16.253 --> 00:44:18.756
because if you use too much pressure,

00:44:18.756 --> 00:44:20.257
you're gonna cause a lot of damage

00:44:20.257 --> 00:44:21.325
to the surface.

00:44:21.325 --> 00:44:25.062
So to treat it, we just
use moderate pressure.

00:44:25.062 --> 00:44:27.531
We make sure always to go with the grain.

00:44:27.531 --> 00:44:30.868
This keeps us from
furrowing/damaging the wood.

00:44:45.516 --> 00:44:47.651
The driveway is terrible.

00:44:47.651 --> 00:44:49.386
Covered in mildew and algae.

00:44:49.386 --> 00:44:51.321
The chemicals we normally use is called

00:44:51.321 --> 00:44:53.991
sodium hypochlorite 12.5%,

00:44:53.991 --> 00:44:58.495
or better known as pool
shock/industrial bleach.

00:44:58.495 --> 00:44:59.897
We normally dilute that down to

00:44:59.897 --> 00:45:02.232
a 50:50 ratio in our buckets.

00:45:02.232 --> 00:45:04.034
This kills the mildew and algae,

00:45:04.034 --> 00:45:06.270
that top layer, when we pretreat,

00:45:06.270 --> 00:45:08.172
it kills that almost instantly.

00:45:12.843 --> 00:45:14.078
This is called a

00:45:14.078 --> 00:45:16.080
Whisper Wash Big Guy surface cleaner.

00:45:16.080 --> 00:45:17.815
That metal tubing

00:45:17.815 --> 00:45:20.884
actually rotates around on a swivel.

00:45:20.884 --> 00:45:22.686
That's the item that I use

00:45:22.686 --> 00:45:24.254
to actually clean concrete.

00:45:40.070 --> 00:45:42.005
From there, we'll actually go around

00:45:42.005 --> 00:45:44.408
washing the trim of around the brick

00:45:44.408 --> 00:45:47.411
and other areas the surface
cleaner couldn't reach.

00:45:47.411 --> 00:45:48.746
And then we will post-treat

00:45:48.746 --> 00:45:49.947
with a detergent again

00:45:49.947 --> 00:45:53.417
to get any organic stains
that are left behind.

00:45:58.622 --> 00:45:59.690
After we do the driveway,

00:45:59.690 --> 00:46:03.327
we'll then do the front
patio and sidewalk.

00:46:03.327 --> 00:46:05.295
Also sometimes if the client wants,

00:46:05.295 --> 00:46:08.198
we'll also do the curves along the drive.

00:46:39.997 --> 00:46:41.698
The house is in pretty good condition,

00:46:41.698 --> 00:46:43.367
but there was a few spots that we noticed

00:46:43.367 --> 00:46:44.234
that were pretty bad

00:46:44.234 --> 00:46:47.404
with heavy and thick mildew and algae.

00:46:47.404 --> 00:46:49.540
On a house we use roughly about

00:46:49.540 --> 00:46:52.409
10% sodium hypochlorite

00:46:52.409 --> 00:46:55.646
mixed with about 90% water.

00:46:55.646 --> 00:46:56.713
So, the black on the brick

00:46:56.713 --> 00:46:59.950
is actually just really
thick mildew and algae.

00:46:59.950 --> 00:47:03.020
This is caused by moisture in shade,

00:47:03.020 --> 00:47:06.657
allowing for such big
mildew and algae to grow.

00:47:19.636 --> 00:47:21.471
When we wash a house's exterior,

00:47:21.471 --> 00:47:23.874
we wash the roof, the
outside of the gutters,

00:47:23.874 --> 00:47:25.876
the side of the home, the windows,

00:47:25.876 --> 00:47:28.979
the doors, anything that is on the side

00:47:28.979 --> 00:47:32.382
or attached to the home itself.

00:47:46.163 --> 00:47:47.030
So, we're finished.

00:47:47.030 --> 00:47:48.932
We finally got the job done.

00:47:48.932 --> 00:47:50.434
So, roughly, this job took us

00:47:50.434 --> 00:47:53.570
about four and a half hours.

00:47:53.570 --> 00:47:55.038
Everything looked amazing.

00:47:55.038 --> 00:47:56.807
The client was beyond happy

00:47:56.807 --> 00:47:58.442
with the job that we've done.

00:47:58.442 --> 00:48:01.745
You can clearly see
everything is transformed

00:48:01.745 --> 00:48:03.847
into a new clean state.

00:48:03.847 --> 00:48:05.482
My personal suggestion,

00:48:05.482 --> 00:48:07.184
instead of trying to do it yourself,

00:48:07.184 --> 00:48:09.219
is to always hire a professional,

00:48:09.219 --> 00:48:10.854
because you're dealing
with some very strong

00:48:10.854 --> 00:48:12.723
and dangerous chemicals,

00:48:12.723 --> 00:48:14.091
and it can be termed dangerous

00:48:14.091 --> 00:48:17.094
when in the hands of
someone not experienced.

00:48:22.332 --> 00:48:25.235
Sleep peacefully as Jon from Super Fresh

00:48:25.235 --> 00:48:28.138
takes years off of this mattress' life.

00:48:29.539 --> 00:48:30.607
My name is Jon Bristol,

00:48:30.607 --> 00:48:32.175
and I'm from Trinidad and Tobago.

00:48:32.175 --> 00:48:33.577
I'm the director of Super Fresh,

00:48:33.577 --> 00:48:35.012
and today I'm going to show you

00:48:35.012 --> 00:48:37.114
how we clean very dirty mattresses

00:48:37.114 --> 00:48:39.116
as well as the average dirty ones.

00:48:41.451 --> 00:48:44.755
So, a typical process of
cleaning a mattress is

00:48:44.755 --> 00:48:47.324
we spray on an enzyme-based cleaner,

00:48:47.324 --> 00:48:48.892
and that will break down the dirt

00:48:48.892 --> 00:48:49.993
and the body oils

00:48:49.993 --> 00:48:52.062
and, you know, the incidents.

00:48:52.062 --> 00:48:54.231
And we give that chemical
some time to work.

00:48:54.231 --> 00:48:56.233
After that, there's some agitation,

00:48:56.233 --> 00:48:57.868
also known as scrubbing,

00:48:57.868 --> 00:48:59.569
and in the final stage of the process,

00:48:59.569 --> 00:49:00.737
we use the extractor,

00:49:00.737 --> 00:49:03.540
and that would spray on a
solution of clean water,

00:49:03.540 --> 00:49:05.375
fabric shampoo, and deodorizer

00:49:05.375 --> 00:49:06.610
and vacuum at the same time.

00:49:06.610 --> 00:49:08.412
So it rinses away everything,

00:49:08.412 --> 00:49:10.614
so there isn't any harsh chemicals left

00:49:10.614 --> 00:49:12.149
to interfere with anyone's skin

00:49:12.149 --> 00:49:13.350
or anything afterwards.

00:49:14.918 --> 00:49:17.955
There's going to be a lot
of dust buildup over time.

00:49:17.955 --> 00:49:19.523
There's going to be hair.

00:49:19.523 --> 00:49:21.091
If there are a lot of kids in your home,

00:49:21.091 --> 00:49:22.359
you're going to find urine,

00:49:22.359 --> 00:49:23.694
even if the client doesn't know.

00:49:23.694 --> 00:49:24.928
Sometimes, you know,

00:49:24.928 --> 00:49:26.530
some people eat on their beds and stuff,

00:49:26.530 --> 00:49:29.132
so you find crumbs from various foodstuff.

00:49:34.838 --> 00:49:37.941
All right. So, I actually had no idea

00:49:37.941 --> 00:49:40.344
what to expect when coming to do this job.

00:49:40.344 --> 00:49:43.347
And I'm still not entirely
sure what caused it.

00:49:43.347 --> 00:49:46.450
It may have been some
flooding in the area,

00:49:46.450 --> 00:49:47.451
but I'm not sure.

00:49:47.451 --> 00:49:50.087
So that was probably more
than five years for sure.

00:49:51.688 --> 00:49:53.623
So, right here, I am spraying on

00:49:53.623 --> 00:49:55.993
the enzyme-based cleaner
onto the mattress,

00:49:55.993 --> 00:49:57.461
and I'm looking to see the areas

00:49:57.461 --> 00:50:00.731
where I may have to apply
another coat as we go along.

00:50:02.399 --> 00:50:05.602
What I use right now,
it's a synthetic enzyme.

00:50:05.602 --> 00:50:06.770
I could use it for everything,

00:50:06.770 --> 00:50:07.738
but there are some stains

00:50:07.738 --> 00:50:10.040
that would require additional chemicals

00:50:10.040 --> 00:50:12.142
if you really want to get
them removed visually.

00:50:12.142 --> 00:50:14.678
But the most important
thing for most clients

00:50:14.678 --> 00:50:16.747
is getting the actual thing

00:50:16.747 --> 00:50:18.482
that caused the stain out.

00:50:18.482 --> 00:50:20.050
So we say there may not be

00:50:20.050 --> 00:50:22.352
a visual improvement, but you can be sure

00:50:22.352 --> 00:50:24.488
that you're getting all the nasties out.

00:50:25.956 --> 00:50:28.225
They're usually just regular buildup,

00:50:28.225 --> 00:50:30.861
so just regular dust and dirt

00:50:30.861 --> 00:50:32.562
that accumulate over the years.

00:50:32.562 --> 00:50:34.798
Some clients don't use

00:50:34.798 --> 00:50:36.700
a full three-piece sheet set.

00:50:36.700 --> 00:50:37.768
A lot of clients don't have

00:50:37.768 --> 00:50:39.703
mattress protectors either.

00:50:39.703 --> 00:50:42.406
Using heat is beneficial because

00:50:42.406 --> 00:50:44.508
heat acts almost like
a universal catalyst.

00:50:44.508 --> 00:50:47.210
So heat would help your
chemicals to work faster,

00:50:47.210 --> 00:50:48.512
so it would help the enzyme cleaner

00:50:48.512 --> 00:50:50.280
to break down the stuff faster,

00:50:50.280 --> 00:50:51.815
and it will also help the fabric shampoo

00:50:51.815 --> 00:50:54.051
to latch onto the dirt particles faster.

00:50:54.051 --> 00:50:56.753
So it helps make the cleaning
process more efficient.

00:50:56.753 --> 00:50:59.990
Unfortunately, there's
no way to know for sure

00:50:59.990 --> 00:51:02.125
that you get 100% of it,

00:51:02.125 --> 00:51:03.293
but you want to know
that you're picking up

00:51:03.293 --> 00:51:04.761
as much as possible.

00:51:09.266 --> 00:51:11.368
This mattress was especially dirty,

00:51:11.368 --> 00:51:13.103
so it's either, like, a lot of years,

00:51:13.103 --> 00:51:14.838
probably more than seven or so,

00:51:14.838 --> 00:51:16.440
or a lot of use.

00:51:16.440 --> 00:51:18.241
But I don't think it's any one thing,

00:51:18.241 --> 00:51:20.043
but just a combination of factors

00:51:20.043 --> 00:51:22.245
that leaves it to be that condition.

00:51:22.245 --> 00:51:25.015
I found that you could use
a variable-speed polisher

00:51:25.015 --> 00:51:27.017
and a scrubbing-brush attachment.

00:51:27.017 --> 00:51:30.220
The brush is rotating at about 600 rpm.

00:51:30.220 --> 00:51:32.189
We use the softest brush available,

00:51:32.189 --> 00:51:34.391
because some fabrics are really delicate.

00:51:34.391 --> 00:51:36.159
And we use that to help break down

00:51:36.159 --> 00:51:38.028
the larger debris and stuff

00:51:38.028 --> 00:51:39.496
on the surface of the fabric.

00:51:39.496 --> 00:51:42.332
So it helps break down the
larger pieces of debris,

00:51:42.332 --> 00:51:43.900
'cause sometimes they have buildup

00:51:43.900 --> 00:51:45.168
that is really, really bad,

00:51:45.168 --> 00:51:47.871
and it's actually, like, caked up.

00:51:47.871 --> 00:51:50.774
The general process for removing stains

00:51:50.774 --> 00:51:52.943
is just to isolate where the stain is,

00:51:52.943 --> 00:51:55.879
and I may double-apply the
enzyme-based cleaner to it

00:51:55.879 --> 00:51:58.515
or I may spend a little
longer scrubbing it.

00:51:58.515 --> 00:52:01.585
[water gushing]

00:52:01.585 --> 00:52:04.421
[cleaner buzzing]

00:52:05.989 --> 00:52:07.757
I believe for this one the client told us

00:52:07.757 --> 00:52:09.426
it was probably about two years,

00:52:09.426 --> 00:52:11.661
and it wasn't too bad.

00:52:11.661 --> 00:52:14.231
It was just, like, a cream-ish color,

00:52:14.231 --> 00:52:15.932
but we know the original color was white,

00:52:15.932 --> 00:52:17.667
and so we sprayed it one time

00:52:17.667 --> 00:52:19.636
and we did the scrubbing.

00:52:19.636 --> 00:52:21.138
I mean, you'll always have the extreme.

00:52:21.138 --> 00:52:23.807
Some would be much, much darker brown.

00:52:23.807 --> 00:52:25.609
Some would be much more spotty.

00:52:25.609 --> 00:52:28.278
But the average person
is just a little cream,

00:52:28.278 --> 00:52:31.448
just a little off-white
from where it should be.

00:52:31.448 --> 00:52:33.183
For mattresses, you're
usually going to get

00:52:33.183 --> 00:52:34.384
those kind of colors,

00:52:34.384 --> 00:52:36.653
because even below the surface,

00:52:36.653 --> 00:52:37.787
you still have a lot of debris.

00:52:37.787 --> 00:52:38.922
Your dead skin cells,

00:52:38.922 --> 00:52:40.657
your dirt, and your dust building up.

00:52:40.657 --> 00:52:42.592
So even if the top surface

00:52:42.592 --> 00:52:44.961
doesn't accurately
represent what is hidden

00:52:44.961 --> 00:52:46.496
inside there, you're going to find it

00:52:46.496 --> 00:52:48.331
once we do the extraction.

00:52:48.331 --> 00:52:49.866
[water gushing]

00:52:55.605 --> 00:52:57.007
After a certain amount of years,

00:52:57.007 --> 00:53:00.477
your mattress significantly
increases in weight

00:53:00.477 --> 00:53:02.179
simply due to the dust

00:53:02.179 --> 00:53:03.947
and dead skin cells that collect.

00:53:03.947 --> 00:53:06.149
So, one, it's going to
be more uncomfortable.

00:53:06.149 --> 00:53:07.918
Two, if you're using it regularly,

00:53:07.918 --> 00:53:10.220
some of that is going
to be entering the air.

00:53:10.220 --> 00:53:12.923
So you're going to have
a lower-quality air

00:53:12.923 --> 00:53:15.158
inside the home. It may attract mites

00:53:15.158 --> 00:53:17.194
if there's a lot of moisture around.

00:53:17.194 --> 00:53:18.795
Just overall it's not going to be

00:53:18.795 --> 00:53:20.397
the best way of well-being.

00:53:20.397 --> 00:53:21.998
For people that are asthmatic,

00:53:21.998 --> 00:53:24.267
the buildup of dust could be a problem.

00:53:24.267 --> 00:53:26.203
For people with sensitive skin,

00:53:26.203 --> 00:53:28.238
any buildup on your mattress over time

00:53:28.238 --> 00:53:29.773
could pose a threat in the future

00:53:29.773 --> 00:53:31.608
in terms of having flare-ups.

00:53:31.608 --> 00:53:34.344
If you're taking only
preventative measures,

00:53:34.344 --> 00:53:36.012
I don't think you should need to service

00:53:36.012 --> 00:53:37.647
more than, like, once a year.

00:53:37.647 --> 00:53:40.016
Get it cleaned and then
get a mattress protector,

00:53:40.016 --> 00:53:41.251
because you can always wash that

00:53:41.251 --> 00:53:42.652
every fortnight or every month,

00:53:42.652 --> 00:53:44.087
and you're in a much better position

00:53:44.087 --> 00:53:46.890
than paying me. And I don't mind,

00:53:46.890 --> 00:53:48.291
but you're in a much better position

00:53:48.291 --> 00:53:49.759
for your own well-being.

00:53:50.627 --> 00:53:53.196
Jay from Cleannex takes
us behind the scenes

00:53:53.196 --> 00:53:56.499
of reviving a restaurant's
greasy exhaust system.

00:53:58.969 --> 00:54:00.537
My name is Jay Lopez,

00:54:00.537 --> 00:54:02.439
and I'm the owner of Cleannex.

00:54:03.306 --> 00:54:05.342
Today, I'm gonna be walking you through

00:54:05.342 --> 00:54:07.210
how we clean a kitchen exhaust system

00:54:07.210 --> 00:54:08.979
in a restaurant.

00:54:08.979 --> 00:54:11.881
The purpose of a kitchen exhaust system

00:54:11.881 --> 00:54:15.452
is to extract the fumes from a kitchen

00:54:15.452 --> 00:54:17.220
in a commercial setting.

00:54:17.220 --> 00:54:18.355
In order to remove grease,

00:54:18.355 --> 00:54:20.290
you have to understand grease.

00:54:20.290 --> 00:54:23.193
What holds the oil together is the fat,

00:54:23.193 --> 00:54:26.196
and once the first layer is up,

00:54:26.196 --> 00:54:27.564
it becomes a magnet,

00:54:27.564 --> 00:54:30.200
and it attracts more and more and more.

00:54:31.134 --> 00:54:33.637
Everything is cleaned from top to bottom,

00:54:33.637 --> 00:54:36.172
and you would start
cleaning the fan first.

00:54:36.172 --> 00:54:38.041
We're using a high-end degreaser

00:54:38.041 --> 00:54:40.343
with a little added ingredient,

00:54:40.343 --> 00:54:43.346
potassium hydroxide and sodium hydroxide.

00:54:43.346 --> 00:54:46.483
Those two chemicals
combined create miracles

00:54:46.483 --> 00:54:48.451
in destroying the fat and the grease.

00:54:48.451 --> 00:54:49.986
In our case, we time it

00:54:49.986 --> 00:54:52.489
somewhere between 10 to 15 minutes.

00:54:53.523 --> 00:54:56.926
Once we see that the
grease starts to melt,

00:54:56.926 --> 00:54:58.228
at that point, we know

00:54:58.228 --> 00:55:01.931
we need to apply hot water and remove it,

00:55:01.931 --> 00:55:03.466
because anything longer at that point

00:55:03.466 --> 00:55:05.502
is going to start to destroy the metal.

00:55:06.403 --> 00:55:08.271
Once the fan has been cleaned,

00:55:08.271 --> 00:55:09.939
the next step would be the duct.

00:55:09.939 --> 00:55:13.977
That's the part that
connects the fan to the hood.

00:55:13.977 --> 00:55:16.279
This level is considered extreme.

00:55:16.279 --> 00:55:18.615
That was a fire waiting to happen.

00:55:18.615 --> 00:55:21.751
I would say this is about
a year worth of grease.

00:55:21.751 --> 00:55:24.087
It all depends on the volume of cooking.

00:55:24.087 --> 00:55:26.589
If this is moderate cooking,

00:55:26.589 --> 00:55:28.825
this is about a year's worth of grease.

00:55:28.825 --> 00:55:29.859
That's a lot.

00:55:32.929 --> 00:55:35.799
The duct is cleaned in
a few different steps.

00:55:35.799 --> 00:55:36.966
During this process,

00:55:36.966 --> 00:55:39.235
we use a tool made by Guardian.

00:55:39.235 --> 00:55:43.173
This tool is known as the
EZ Magnetic Duct Scraper.

00:55:43.173 --> 00:55:45.475
It has magnets set into the tool

00:55:45.475 --> 00:55:47.844
and a razor blade on each side.

00:55:48.845 --> 00:55:51.047
Once it's clenched to the duct,

00:55:51.047 --> 00:55:53.983
it is manually moved forward and backwards

00:55:53.983 --> 00:55:56.019
until the grease is removed.

00:55:56.019 --> 00:55:57.654
This forward-and-backward method

00:55:57.654 --> 00:55:59.189
assures that the grease buildup

00:55:59.189 --> 00:56:02.292
will be removed from both sides.

00:56:02.292 --> 00:56:04.627
It will go from the yellow color

00:56:04.627 --> 00:56:07.263
all the way into the black.

00:56:07.263 --> 00:56:08.798
When it's in the black level,

00:56:08.798 --> 00:56:10.834
that's an extreme level.

00:56:10.834 --> 00:56:12.902
That means that the system

00:56:12.902 --> 00:56:15.872
has not been properly maintained.

00:56:17.207 --> 00:56:18.708
In these types of designs,

00:56:18.708 --> 00:56:21.745
we'll use duct spinners and duct brushes.

00:56:21.745 --> 00:56:24.881
Duct spinner is designed to self-rotate

00:56:24.881 --> 00:56:26.750
within a protective cage

00:56:26.750 --> 00:56:28.952
and spray water at high speed,

00:56:28.952 --> 00:56:32.222
which dislodges buildup
from the metal duct.

00:56:33.957 --> 00:56:35.558
We use a combination

00:56:35.558 --> 00:56:38.495
of a foaming agent and a degreaser.

00:56:38.495 --> 00:56:41.164
The foam is used as a delivering agent,

00:56:41.164 --> 00:56:43.800
and that allows the
degreaser to stay in contact

00:56:43.800 --> 00:56:46.302
with the grease for a longer period.

00:56:46.302 --> 00:56:48.004
If foam was not used,

00:56:48.004 --> 00:56:50.440
the degreaser would drip off the grease.

00:56:54.077 --> 00:56:57.614
The next step would be
the hood and the filters.

00:56:57.614 --> 00:57:00.383
See, the filters are your
first line of defense.

00:57:00.383 --> 00:57:04.053
What the filters is doing
is holding back heat

00:57:04.053 --> 00:57:07.023
and is holding back most of that grease.

00:57:07.023 --> 00:57:09.893
When the filters no
longer can hold the grease

00:57:09.893 --> 00:57:13.863
and once that grease starts
getting heat and melting,

00:57:13.863 --> 00:57:15.331
you have the potential of that grease

00:57:15.331 --> 00:57:17.000
dropping into your food.

00:57:17.000 --> 00:57:19.002
And at that point, it's a health issue.

00:57:19.002 --> 00:57:20.970
I've seen restaurants been shut down

00:57:20.970 --> 00:57:22.105
by the health department

00:57:22.105 --> 00:57:23.540
for not cleaning their hood.

00:57:23.540 --> 00:57:25.608
I've seen restaurants been shut down

00:57:25.608 --> 00:57:27.510
by the fire marshal

00:57:27.510 --> 00:57:29.612
for not maintaining their hoods.

00:57:31.881 --> 00:57:33.116
We take precaution

00:57:33.116 --> 00:57:35.985
in protecting the area from contamination.

00:57:35.985 --> 00:57:38.488
We use two layers of plastic sheeting

00:57:38.488 --> 00:57:40.590
in order to protect the appliances

00:57:40.590 --> 00:57:43.193
from coming in contact with any chemical.

00:57:43.193 --> 00:57:45.528
The second layer of plastic sheeting

00:57:45.528 --> 00:57:47.831
is wrapped around the hood,

00:57:47.831 --> 00:57:49.966
and then a funnel is created.

00:57:49.966 --> 00:57:53.470
The funnel is used to
drain the chemical grease

00:57:53.470 --> 00:57:56.005
and water into the plastic containers.

00:57:57.807 --> 00:58:00.243
The plastic sheeting also serves

00:58:00.243 --> 00:58:01.978
as a protective barrier

00:58:01.978 --> 00:58:03.980
in preventing the chemical from

00:58:03.980 --> 00:58:07.884
coming in contact with
the food-preparation area.

00:58:08.751 --> 00:58:10.019
Once we're completed

00:58:10.019 --> 00:58:12.121
and the plastic sheeting is removed,

00:58:12.121 --> 00:58:14.924
we'll conduct a freshwater wipe down

00:58:14.924 --> 00:58:18.027
and recommend to the client
that they do the same

00:58:18.027 --> 00:58:21.498
prior to preparing any food.

00:58:21.498 --> 00:58:24.167
According to NFPA 96,

00:58:24.167 --> 00:58:27.670
which is the agency that
regulates this industry,

00:58:27.670 --> 00:58:29.272
the frequency of cleanings

00:58:29.272 --> 00:58:32.175
depends on the style of
cooking you're conducting

00:58:32.175 --> 00:58:34.043
and the frequency of cooking.

00:58:34.043 --> 00:58:36.779
For example, if you cook with solid fuel,

00:58:36.779 --> 00:58:39.949
solid fuel is basically
cooking with charcoal.

00:58:39.949 --> 00:58:42.986
Those are 30-day mandatory cleanings,

00:58:42.986 --> 00:58:46.422
because they produce a lot more grease,

00:58:46.422 --> 00:58:50.026
and the embers that
come from the solid fuel

00:58:50.026 --> 00:58:51.761
can ignite the grease.

00:58:51.761 --> 00:58:54.564
If you're an occasional cooking facility,

00:58:54.564 --> 00:58:56.366
those would be a year.

